    "granted_utc": "2026-08-22T19:25:19Z",
    "granted_by": "Maintainer decision, 2026-08-23. 'GO for Recovery Continuation Attempt 5. I reviewed c3116f2. Both Attempt-4 memory-contract blockers are closed sufficiently for one further paid continuation. The release lifecycle is now correct: the owning frame snapshots CUDA state, explicitly drops teacher and evaluator, and only then calls complete_release(before). require_released() makes genuine live retention fail closed before recovery. Keep that contract unchanged. The new RECOVERY_TRAINER_BYTES basis is also accepted. The 39.79 GiB peak comes from completed 1023-step runs of the same memory-relevant recovery recipe on an L40S under the same /opt/train torch 2.11.0+cu128 environment used by the continuation. The Attempt-4 reserved and non-PyTorch terms convert that observed PyTorch peak into the 41.65 GiB device requirement, and the existing 2 GiB headroom margin remains intact.' Against last tested implementation c3116f2, which is also the pre-authorization HEAD. Since attempt 4, two closures landed and were accepted, both inside the 22-file harness. (1) THE HANDOFF LIFECYCLE: release_to_subprocess(drop=[...]) was itself the defect -- it copied the caller's sequence into a callee-local list and cleared that, which cannot rebind a caller's name, and it measured `after` before the caller's del ran, so a real release could not register. complete_release(before) now accepts NO objects; the caller snapshots with cuda_memory(), deletes its own names, then measures. Applied identically to the continuation's teacher/evaluator path and full Phase A's found path, with `evaluator` in the del because StateEvaluator.prime_reference stores the teacher on _teacher. require_released() makes the live_retention verdict block above the same 1 GiB limit, kept separate from require_headroom so a caller can tell whether the release failed or the card is merely full. (2) THE TRAINER REQUIREMENT: RECOVERY_TRAINER_BYTES was 22 GiB, chosen from attempt 12's mid-failure footprint and 17.79 GiB below the trainer's real peak. Attempt 4's 36.30 GiB is only a lower bound because it OOM'd inside the first kd_forward_kl, before backward() and before the first AdamW.step(). The permanent controls preflight_ctl_r0860k_{sa,sb} each report max_memory_allocated() of 39.79 GiB over COMPLETED 1023-step runs on an L40S, reproducing every memory-relevant field of the frozen recipe. Converted to a device requirement with attempt 4's own reserved and non-PyTorch terms: 41.65 GiB. No calibration launch was purchased. Verified at $0 on the current tree: full suite 2172 passed / 12 skipped, +10 on 2162 with the skip count unchanged; frozen-asset verifier passed with no problems; 7 mutations each turning a passing state into a failing one. The continuation harness digest moved 162c09ed -> 95cf336d.\n\nCovers: One (1) launch of the AutoInitializer Phase-A RECOVERY CONTINUATION, attempt 5, on a single NVIDIA L40S at no more than $0.99/h. It imports the verified Attempt-12 Stage-1 result -- the five preserved selected leaves, in the committed selected order, re-identified from the bytes that actually arrive on the pod -- and runs recovery Stages 2 through 5 under the frozen science, the existing watchdog, soft-stop, hard-stop, artifact-gate and provider-confirmed teardown behaviour. The reviewed execution path is preserved exactly: Stage 0 attestation, strict Stage-1 import, canonical-control measurement once on the frozen Stage-1 suite, admit_leaves over the five imported leaves plus the measured control, the corrected device handoff with require_released and require_headroom, then frozen recovery Stages 2-5 only, with the conditional seed-sc rung firing only if the frozen rule fires. Attempt 4 already demonstrated Stages 0 and 1 on paid hardware, and attempts 3 and 4 demonstrated the transport; none of that is revisited. Authorized pricing is DERIVED from continuation_budget(), not written: expected $14.9233, per-launch hard ceiling $16.7456, which is also the hard cap. The beam search is not run, not reconstructed, and is structurally unreachable from this harness.\n\nCumulative spend at approval $214.3326 against a cumulative cap of $234.00.\n\nDoes NOT authorize: Lowering RECOVERY_TRAINER_BYTES, weakening or removing require_released or require_headroom, or changing the release lifecycle, in order to make a launch succeed or to avoid a refusal. Altering the frozen recovery recipe, KD chunk, microbatch, dtype, optimizer or trainer implementation. Revisiting Stage 1, the transport, leaf selection or committed order, the control-measurement design, or the recovery science. A beam search, a fresh Stage 1, or any reconstruction of the search. Retraining the permanent controls. Measuring the canonical control more than once or on anything but the frozen Stage-1 suite. Any new canary, rehearsal, readiness, measurement, calibration, architecture, pricing or harness expansion. Any additional storage cleanup, including the 32 sole-copy remote objects totalling 69.671 GiB or the two protected wheelhouses. Any live-pod repair followed by a relaunch. Increasing the cumulative cap. Any follow-on or automatically started session. Reopening the frozen science: science plan 02be33b9, session plan 9377a2dc, Stage-3 protocol 250f72ef, equivalence interval 0.011695296982299022, feasibility floor 0.3, tokenizer 7781771acc3798ee, recovery fingerprint ab0d8cfd, the seed and conditional-sc policy, the operator ids and declared mathematics, or the recovery, search and selection rules. Reusing any spent grant or authorization from attempts 1 through 4. Any other paid session. Treating the remaining cumulative headroom as authorization beyond this one launch.\n\nGrant document: logs/autoinit_recovery_continuation_attempt5_grant.json sha256 3494c911232ab0ad53ee04314b7f3984fae38b4072ce572efba759c1cefbd82a",
